      "answer_status": "MTS Model Response",
      "credit_value": 4,
      "model_response": "Both foxes trick other animals to get food and make their plans sound appealing. The first Fox wants the Crow’s cheese, so he flatters her and suggests her singing could make her Queen of Birds. When she opens her beak the cheese falls. The second Fox wants a share of the pike, so he gains the otters’ trust by claiming to be fair and uses his position as judge to take most of the fish. One trick exploits vanity and the other exploits a disagreement. (Crow ¶1–2, 5–6; Otters ¶2–5, 8–13)",
